+# Reactive Spreadsheet Engine
+
+A spreadsheet engine in TypeScript: a grid of cells holding values or formulas,
+where changing a cell automatically recomputes everything that depends on it.
+
+```ts
+import { Spreadsheet } from './src/index.js';
+
+const sheet = new Spreadsheet();
+
+sheet.set('A1', 10);
+sheet.set('A2', 20);
+sheet.set('A3', '=A1+A2');
+sheet.get('A3');        // 30
+sheet.getDisplay('A3'); // "30"
+
+sheet.set('A1', 50);
+sheet.get('A3');        // 70  — recomputed automatically
+
+sheet.set('B1', '=SUM(A1:A2)');   // 70
+sheet.set('C1', '=IF(A1>30, "big", "small")');  // "big"
+```
+
+## API
+
+| Method | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `set(cell, value)` | Write a value, or a formula when the string starts with `=`. Writing `''` clears the cell. |
+| `get(cell)` | Computed value, or `null` when the cell is empty. |
+| `getDisplay(cell)` | Computed value rendered as a string. |
+| `getCells()` | `Map` of every non-empty cell to its computed value. |
+| `getFormula(cell)` | The text originally written, so formulas can be read back. |
+| `clear(cell)` / `clearAll()` / `has(cell)` | Remove one cell, empty the sheet, test occupancy. |
+
+Cell addresses are case-insensitive and columns run `A`–`Z`, `AA`–`AZ`, `BA`… .
+
+## Formula language
+
+Arithmetic `+ - * / ^`, parentheses, comparisons `> < >= <= = <>`, and the
+functions `SUM`, `AVERAGE`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, `IF`, plus `AND`, `OR`, `NOT`.
+Ranges are `A1:A5` (column), `A1:C1` (row) or `A1:C3` (block); the corners may be
+given in either order. Function and range names are case-insensitive.
+
+## How reactivity works
+
+Each formula's precedents are extracted at parse time — ranges are expanded to
+the individual cells they cover, so a formula over `A1:A100` still reacts when
+`A50` is filled in later. The engine keeps the reverse edges too.
+
+A write recomputes the cell and its transitive dependents **eagerly**, ordered by
+Kahn's algorithm over the affected subgraph. Every affected cell is therefore
+evaluated exactly once, no matter how many paths lead to it, and reads are a plain
+map lookup. A chain of repeated diamonds (`A(n) = A(n-1) + A(n-2)`) that would take
+exponential time under naive edge-following stays linear here; `src/scale.test.ts`
+covers that case.
+
+## Two kinds of error
+
+Problems with the **edit** throw, and leave the sheet untouched:
+
+- `CircularReferenceError` — the write would make a cell depend on itself. The
+  check walks existing edges *before* mutating anything, and carries the path so
+  the message names the cycle (`A1 -> B1 -> A1`).
+- `FormulaSyntaxError` — the formula does not parse.
+- `InvalidReferenceError` — the address is not a valid cell reference.
+
+Problems with a **computation** become in-band values that propagate through
+dependent formulas, exactly as a real spreadsheet does — `get` returns the code as
+a string:
+
+`#DIV/0!` (division by zero, or `AVERAGE` of nothing) · `#VALUE!` (a value of the
+wrong type) · `#NAME?` (unknown function) · `#NUM!` (overflow or an undefined
+result).
+
+An error clears itself as soon as its cause is fixed.
+
+## Semantics worth knowing
+
+These follow Excel where the specification is silent:
+
+- Empty cells read as `0` in arithmetic, `false` in conditions, and `''` when
+  compared against text.
+- Aggregates over a **range** use only numeric cells, skipping text, booleans and
+  blanks — so `AVERAGE` divides by the count of numbers, not the size of the range.
+  A value written **directly** as an argument is coerced instead, so
+  `SUM(A1:A3, "5", TRUE)` adds 5 and 1.
+- `MIN`, `MAX` and `COUNT` of an empty range are `0`; `AVERAGE` of one is `#DIV/0!`.
+- `IF` evaluates only the branch it takes, so the untaken branch may safely divide
+  by zero. Both branches still count as dependencies.
+- Across types, numbers sort below text, which sorts below booleans. Text
+  comparison is case-**sensitive**, which departs from Excel in favour of the
+  behaviour a TypeScript caller would expect.
+- `get` returns full floating-point precision; `getDisplay` trims to 15
+  significant digits, so `0.1 + 0.2` displays as `0.3`.
+
+## Development
+
+```bash
+npm test           # vitest, 135 tests
+npm run build      # tsc -> dist/
+npm run lint       # eslint
+```
+
+Source lives in `src/`: `tokenizer` → `parser` (→ `ast`) → `evaluator`, with
+`reference` for address arithmetic, `values` for coercion and formatting,
+`errors` for the two error families, and `spreadsheet` for the dependency graph.
